  {
    "role": "user",
    "content": {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Triage the workspace. Steps:\n1. Call `query` with `{\"kind\":\"blocked\"}` to find blocked issues, and `dep` with `{\"action\":\"cycles\"}` to surface any dependency cycles.\n2. Call `query` with `{\"kind\":\"count\",\"group_by\":\"assignee\"}` to size unassigned work (issues with no assignee or an empty one count under the key `\"\"`; a spaces-only assignee gets its own key), then `{\"kind\":\"list\"}` and keep the issues whose `assignee` is absent or blank (empty or spaces only). No filter selects every unassigned issue: `assignee` matches one exact value, and omitting it (or passing null) matches all issues.\n3. Call `query` with `{\"kind\":\"stale\",\"older_than\":\"<RFC 3339 timestamp 30 days before now>\"}` for issues not updated in the last 30 days.\n4. Find deferred work. It takes two forms. The `defer` tool sets a `defer_until` timestamp and leaves the status unchanged (usually `open`): in the step-2 list, keep the issues whose `defer_until` is in the future. An issue whose status was set to `deferred` is hidden from `list`, `count`, `stale` and `search` by default (`blocked` shows it only when it is blocked): call `query` with `{\"kind\":\"list\",\"status\":[\"deferred\"],\"include_deferred\":true}` (without `include_deferred` this returns nothing).\n5. For each blocked issue, inspect its `dep` `tree` and propose the next unblocking action.\n6. Summarise: what is blocked, unassigned, stale and deferred, and the highest-leverage next step."
    }
  }
]
